  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1253 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nSaturday-Sunday...The warm front near the Highway 34 corridor \nfinally moves further north into the CWA, spawning a smaller \nround of isolated showers and storms (20-30% coverage) on \nSaturday. As the boundary becomes diffuse Saturday night, dry \nconditions return as southeasterly flow advects in more mild air\nfor Sunday. Attention turns west though as a longwave trough \npropagates eastward into the Rockies, setting the stage for a \npotentially active start to the work week. \n\nMonday-Wednesday...As the longwave trough shifts eastward into \nthe Plains, there are two potential scenarios:\n\nScenario 1 (70% chance per LREF cluster analysis): The troughs \nin the polar jet and subtropical jet become out of phase, \nleading to a slower, closed-heights center over the south- \ncentral Plains and a fast northern trough. This would support \ntwo low pressure centers: one over the Great Lakes and east- \ncentral Canada and another across the Southern Plains and Lower \nMississippi River Valley. With a split system, rainfall amounts \nare likely to be lower.\n\nScenario 2 (30% chance per LREF cluster analysis): The troughs \nstay in phase, deepening a singular mid-latitude cyclone over \nthe Central Plains that would move into the Mid-Mississippi \nRiver Valley. This solution would favor more rainfall and \nwindier conditions for our CWA.\n\nRegardless of scenario though, precipitation chances and winds \nare forecasted to increase heading into Monday night and stick \naround into Wednesday. With the background cyclonic flow aloft, \nthere could be multiple rounds of rainfall over this time \nperiod. Once the mid-latitude cyclone or an associated cold \nfront moves through or east of the CWA, northerly flow should \nset in and advect in some cooler, more seasonable air by the \nmiddle to end of next week.",
